  {
    "id": "b88ecd16f0c0813c27e1888ad939cd24a5d13717",
    "individual_note": true,
    "resolvable": false,
    "notes": [
      {
        "id": 3957746597,
        "type": null,
        "body": "Cause named: the test's fixture, not the migrations. In review as !24 (phase 2 of the plan, tag `object-save-fixes-p2`).\n\nThe fixture hides `pet_data`, `pet_save_objs` and `schema_migrations` behind temporary tables and records migration 2026091007 as applied, because InnoDB refuses a foreign key on a temporary table. `verify_pet_persistence_schema()` reads the key from `information_schema`, which lists the permanent tables only. The test passed only where the syntax-check boot, or the live-schema test later in the suite, had already put the key on the permanent `pet_save_objs`.\n\nChecked with the repro above: the test alone leaves the permanent tables untouched; with the key added to the permanent table by hand and nothing else changed it passes; and `Test_pet_live_schema_cascades_objects_and_rejects_orphans` alone on a fresh `master_schema.sql` database runs all fifteen migrations on the permanent tables and verifies, so a fresh install's first boot is not affected.\n\nThe fix is in the test: it runs the pet migrations on the permanent tables before it creates the temporary ones. It then passes alone on a `master_schema.sql`-only database, with the syntax-check boot skipped and after it.",
        "author": {
          "id": 42759441,
          "username": "max757",
          "public_email": "",
          "name": "Max aka Mosheh",
          "state": "active",
          "locked": false,
          "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/3c7c169343bd7d0f4575137ce8065aee7cb44024d447bbfe66d773133a81c43e?s=80&d=identicon",
          "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/max757"
        },
        "created_at": "2026-10-05T18:49:32.635Z",
        "updated_at": "2026-10-05T18:49:32.635Z",
        "system": false,
        "noteable_id": 205379580,
        "noteable_type": "Issue",
